#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/id #uuid "51d10b95-2ba8-5125-8f0d-b21455269c1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9419" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = -3xy^2.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y > 0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <                0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<         0$ | $x> 0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-3x$ | $y^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/743df5f5c9232346866a01aed9afa3d41fff0f61bf7812b7ae5db9a43efe59f4.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > 0$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <                0$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<         0$ | $x> 0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128973 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "490982ac-6197-58eb-8d94-2ef28d659d50" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/id #uuid "0dea7752-3eb9-58d5-bdd9-50ec96e8d9b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9423" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y) = -6x^2y.$ Determine the region that gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y) \\geq 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-6x^2$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/17dd9e10234b6a69123241a882e267d95d505d1944d7c31ef563f9ac06b990af.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6150292f-2307-5654-a323-06e21e2d3843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287058} {:answer/id #uuid "09570e86-4e5f-5561-887e-2f85fd3b5ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287059} {:answer/id #uuid "9e7ab2eb-2689-519e-aab4-0bb843910242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287060} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0490ae-f0b6-528a-b3fd-251cd2eeff89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287061} {:answer/id #uuid "c698be13-fc85-5edb-beed-85245d2c0cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba0490ae-f0b6-528a-b3fd-251cd2eeff89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287061} :answer-field/id #uuid "400193f1-2e56-5935-8140-ff92cfb66e41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "044d4f72-bfa3-5995-a255-529e2a3f6304" :question/math-academy-id "q-121237" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/c35c98904d2a0d0d6d49c582cd9f9dba9d96e91ae0da5deb2d329f38f77532e3.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/9340582eaad37c4ce1e0c8cbcdce48faec1e36c3c05adbedd61aa2de7168fd2f.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $2{x}^{2}$ | $y$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/7c851189a040934728e1b312706b4727c141cc678a4be870b6057e281be6e0fd.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ad027d7-b9b1-5505-b671-a96dfc6cf952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287065} {:answer/id #uuid "2ba810d4-2286-5118-bc6c-d0ff57ca25b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |