le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/c44c38febc908fe5e941978bf44bab2e2cb4bd250765950d84f9294c9c4c9c4b.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/209576cd78e9beb1c0f7a9bce8267163df1668a830ffc5c06918f8a726600fbe.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | ${y}^{3}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n  \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004890/assets/433e949488dc7d20c4fda4d508941da3a12363750b7bf58eada642b98f130eb2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "798800a5-255a-5738-91d2-21c2a78e4fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ab8625-6abd-5b84-9128-945e67d0fce3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287121} {:answer/id #uuid "a7dc5479-09ca-5035-8446-797ffbdbd5a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7dc5479-09ca-5035-8446-797ffbdbd5a7" :answer/type {:db/id